 loops); in fact, Frucht shows that there are infinitely many possibilities for \\(\\Gamma\\).\n[7].\n\nThe symmetry group of \\(\\Lambda\\) acts on the feasible region \\(\\{x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid Ax \\le b\\}\\) and on the set of all solutions \\(x\\) of \\(\\Lambda\\). One can show that a solvable linear program \\(\\Lambda\\) has always a solution which is fixed by the symmetry group of \\(\\Lambda\\); see [4], compare also [8].\n\nIn all our constructions and examples we always use the utility vector \\(c = (1,1,\\ldots,1)^t\\); then the condition \\(c^g = c\\) is automatically satisfied, and \\(\\Lambda\\) is described by specifying only the linear constraints \\(Ax \\le b\\).\n\n# 3 All finite groups occur\n\nThe following results extend the list of ‘group-universal’ structures in the sense of Funk, Kegel and Strambach [3] (see also [1]).\n\n**Theorem 1.** *Every abstract finite group is the full symmetry group of a suitable linear program.*\n\n**Proof.** Choose \\(\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}\\). According to a result of Frucht [2], every finite group \\(G\\) is isomorphic to the full automorphism group of some finite graph \\(\\Gamma\\) (which is undirected and free of loops); in fact, Frucht shows that there are infinitely many possibilities for \\(\\Gamma\\). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWolfgang D. Knapp - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/Person%3A304567)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Knapp ... Journal of Group Theory  | 2026-03-05  | Paper ... Theorem B of Hall-Higman revisited ... Codes with prescribed permutation group\nJournal of Algebra  | 1981-01-01  | Paper\nCodes with prescribed permutation group\nJournal of Algebra  | 1980-01-01  | Paper\nEin Faktorisierungssatz für endliche Gruppen und eine Anwendung\nArchiv der Mathematik  | 1980-01-01  | Paper\nON PRIMITIVE PERMUTATION GROUPS INDUCING A FROBENIUS GROUP ON A SUBORBIT, II\nTh